# What wztrun produces. The source is the project; the binary is what you get from it,
# and a committed binary is the one thing you can never take back out of a history.
/bin/

# data/ IS committed, on purpose. The tickets in it are this project's own backlog -- every
# decision made while building it, including the ones that were wrong -- and the README and
# the documentation both point at it as the thing you should read. Ignoring it would make a
# fresh clone open on an empty board and turn that claim into a lie.
#
# It does mean your own tickets show up in `git status` once you start using it. Empty the
# directory if you want a board of your own.

# The contact sheet the screenshot tooling writes, so a whole set can be reviewed in one
# page. It is in Dutch, it is for us, and nothing in the documentation links to it.
/docs/img/index.html
